WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:06.950
Chinese mythology is a rich and varied tradition with influences from Confucianism, Taoism,

00:00:06.950 --> 00:00:08.310
and Buddhism.

00:00:08.310 --> 00:00:13.180
Similar to other cultural mythologies, the tales were often considered actual history,

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:18.430
featuring villains and heroes, and some beings that defy categorization.&nbsp;

00:00:18.430 --> 00:00:23.960
One such figure is the Taotie —the most enduring of the “Four Evils” of Chinese

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:25.369
myth.

00:00:25.369 --> 00:00:27.420
&nbsp;
Despite the consistent descriptions of sharp

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:33.260
teeth, strong claws, and a notoriously voracious appetite, this monster’s genesis remains

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:34.629
elusive.

00:00:34.629 --> 00:00:39.600
Yet it also happens to share a name with one of the most famous motifs in ancient Chinese

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:40.649
art.

00:00:40.649 --> 00:00:46.249
So how did the bulging eyes and gaping mouth of the taotie design on Bronze vessels turn

00:00:46.249 --> 00:00:49.690
into the taotie monster that still intrigues us today?

00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:54.620
Did I also mention it represents greed and gluttony?

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:58.620
[Monstrum intro.]

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:05.080
Long before its appearance in written history, the taotie existed in another documented form—ancient

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:06.330
Chinese art.

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:10.360
From the very start of the Bronze Age [c.3000 BCE] the taotie featured prominently

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:16.510
in the patterns, lines, and ornaments of bronze items—precursors to written storytelling.

00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:21.920
These “beast-face” designs—later called the taotie motif—were symmetrical representations

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:27.720
of non-human creatures that famously dominated bronze art as early as the Shang Dynasty (c.

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:33.180
1600-1045 BC) and enduring into the Zhou dynasties (c.1045-256 BCE)—from roughly 1600 to 256

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:34.410
BCE.

00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:39.480
Often these faces were rendered using a multiple perspective approach, combining the profile

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:44.450
views of other animals to form a central zoomorphic image or beast.

00:01:44.450 --> 00:01:50.400
The style is defined by its two, round bulging eyes, a gaping mouth that often featured no

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:54.590
lower jaw, heavy eyebrows, ears, and sometimes horns.

00:01:54.590 --> 00:01:56.950
&nbsp;
Naturally, there are no written records of

00:01:56.950 --> 00:02:02.090
what this bronze age iconography was called when they were created or what purpose the

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:03.690
designs served.

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:08.989
Archeologists, historians, and other scholars have pieced together plausible explanations

00:02:08.989 --> 00:02:14.159
and generated likely narratives, but as with all ancient scholarship, this leaves ample

00:02:14.159 --> 00:02:16.590
room for variations and disagreement.

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:22.939
In fact, many scholars argue that the taotie design originated far earlier, in Neolithic

00:02:22.939 --> 00:02:29.159
times, citing images of the taotie beast-face across multiple artistic forms, including

00:02:29.159 --> 00:02:31.019
jade objects.

00:02:31.019 --> 00:02:37.189
Either way, the taotie beast-face is one of the earliest monstrous forms found in Chinese art.

00:02:37.189 --> 00:02:40.900
&nbsp;So what might the taotie have represented?&nbsp;

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:45.389
Experts present several theories, many of which revolve around the ancient Chinese religious

00:02:45.389 --> 00:02:48.250
tradition of ancestor worship.

00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:53.489
Some argue that taotie designs depict the animal helpers of male and female shamans,

00:02:53.489 --> 00:02:57.249
who aided communication between the living and the dead.

00:02:57.249 --> 00:03:02.580
Other scholars assert that they are a depiction of masks that were worn to symbolize ancestor

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:07.939
spirits, and still others diverge a bit and
suggest that the taotie may represent even

00:03:07.939 --> 00:03:11.099
older beliefs in nature deities and spirits.

00:03:11.099 --> 00:03:13.609
&nbsp;
Of course there are some scholars who say

00:03:13.609 --> 00:03:18.689
it’s just an ornamental pattern and not
much else-- but where’s the fun in that?

00:03:18.689 --> 00:03:24.109
This theory ignores the very history of how the bronze vessels came to be, who commissioned

00:03:24.109 --> 00:03:27.999
them, the artisans who made them, and why.

00:03:27.999 --> 00:03:32.640
Bronze Age artisans created these Chinese vessels under the employment of rich and powerful

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:35.680
patrons, often royalty and nobility.

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:41.400
Thus, the taotie, a frequent figure on such items, might also have served as a status

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:44.930
symbol meant to evoke the owner’s social class.

00:03:44.930 --> 00:03:49.060
This interpretation holds up, especially when you consider that beginning in the Warring

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:53.560
States period, the word “taotie” came
to mean “glutton.”

00:03:53.560 --> 00:03:56.269
&nbsp;
Further, many of these bronze vessels were

00:03:56.269 --> 00:04:01.730
commissioned not for just any wealthy patrons, but for the Shang Dynasty kings themselves.

00:04:01.730 --> 00:04:07.319
These kings —believed to have gods as their ancestors —maintained their power through

00:04:07.319 --> 00:04:11.090
conquests in battle and successful harvests.

00:04:11.090 --> 00:04:16.180
Sacrificial offerings to the kings’ ancestral deities and to the supreme deity, Di, were

00:04:16.180 --> 00:04:21.350
collected from lesser nobles in the form of processions, spoils from hunts, and animal

00:04:21.350 --> 00:04:23.320
and human sacrifice.

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:29.320
The bronze vessels used in such tributes—ones that were adorned with early images of taotie—were

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:34.920
of utmost importance, being fundamental to the rituals, and thus instrumental in politics

00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:36.510
and even the overall economy.&nbsp;

00:04:36.510 --> 00:04:40.420
&nbsp;
In fact, bronze-making skills were honed almost

00:04:40.420 --> 00:04:46.360
exclusively around ornamental pursuits, rather than functional efforts like weapons or agricultural

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:47.600
tools.

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:52.900
And so we can infer that the image of the taotie, with its fangs, horns, and large eyes,

00:04:52.900 --> 00:04:58.350
was an integral component of early Chinese ceremonial and religious life.

00:04:58.350 --> 00:05:03.870
Over time, taotie bronze vessels developed connections to food for humans too.

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:05.880
The feasts of ancient China were notorious.

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:10.670
In the Zhou Dynasty (c. 1045-256 BC) after the Bronze Age, it was estimated that for

00:05:10.670 --> 00:05:17.150
some time 60% of the ruling kings’ servants were dedicated to food- related duties, even

00:05:17.150 --> 00:05:22.410
as the majority of the population lived with chronic deprivation and even starvation.

00:05:22.410 --> 00:05:28.300
The filling of a bronze vessel with sacrificial food or food for a feast associated the taotie

00:05:28.300 --> 00:05:33.170
motif with abundance and underscored that having enough to share was a sign of good

00:05:33.170 --> 00:05:34.970
manners and social inclusion.&nbsp;

00:05:34.970 --> 00:05:39.230
&nbsp;
The association of the word taotie with gluttony

00:05:39.230 --> 00:05:44.400
did not emerge until after this period, in
the last three periods of the Zhou dynasty,

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:49.840
when the outbreak of war meant feeding one’s soldiers became more of a priority than feasting,

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:52.650
and further strain was placed on food supplies.

00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:57.540
The taotie motif on the bronze vessels may have then been seen as a sign of extravagance--something

00:05:57.540 --> 00:05:59.780
to be avoided during wartime.&nbsp;

00:05:59.780 --> 00:06:05.500
It’s no surprise, then, that the artistic
rendering of the taotie face ended up inspiring

00:06:05.500 --> 00:06:11.860
mythology in the form of the Taotie monster, one of the four “legendary monsters” or

00:06:11.860 --> 00:06:14.060
malevolences of Chinese mythology.

00:06:14.060 --> 00:06:16.530
&nbsp;In Chinese mythology, depictions of monsters

00:06:16.530 --> 00:06:22.280
served an instructive or even protective purpose, and could be portrayed repelling evil or helping

00:06:22.280 --> 00:06:25.240
humans avoid real dangers.

00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:29.460
Creatures like the Taotie were considered real and mythological, capable of occupying

00:06:29.460 --> 00:06:32.260
either or both states.

00:06:32.260 --> 00:06:37.720
That said, the mythological version of Taotie, with its insatiable appetite and incredible

00:06:37.720 --> 00:06:43.380
greed, definitely inspired fear as it offered instruction—I mean, they were said to be

00:06:43.380 --> 00:06:46.440
man-devouring beasts after all.

00:06:46.440 --> 00:06:48.570
&nbsp;Written narratives of the Taotie monster begin

00:06:48.570 --> 00:06:54.280
to appear around the 4th century BCE, starting with the The Zuo Tradition or Zuo’s Commentary

00:06:54.280 --> 00:06:58.960
(Zuo zhuan), which also happens to be the first complete narrative work in Chinese literature.

00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:01.480
&nbsp;As the story goes, in ancient times two

00:07:01.480 --> 00:07:06.860
of the ruling gods had eight virtuous sons each, and three other gods had one worthless

00:07:06.860 --> 00:07:10.800
son each: Hundun (‘Chaos’), Qiongqi (‘Completely Devious’), and Taowu (‘Benighted Trouble’).

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:16.100
These in turn were the three evils—malevolences—whose maliciousness passed down through

00:07:16.100 --> 00:07:22.080
generations until the time of Emperor Yao,
a legendary ruler in Chinese mythology.

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:26.811
During Yao’s reign, a fourth evil son, “greedy for food and drink” was born to the ancient

00:07:26.811 --> 00:07:28.590
Jinyun clan.

00:07:28.590 --> 00:07:30.890
This one could never be satisfied!

00:07:30.890 --> 00:07:35.080
He hoarded food, refusing to share with even those most desperate for charity.

00:07:35.080 --> 00:07:39.200
He was called Taotie, and became the fourth Malevolence.

00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:43.620
Luckily Emperor Yao’s minister expelled
these Four Malevolences and their clans to

00:07:43.620 --> 00:07:48.700
the four edges of the earth, once in each cardinal direction—tasking them with warding

00:07:48.700 --> 00:07:51.180
off demons and bad spirits.

00:07:51.180 --> 00:07:53.960
&nbsp;Like most myths, there are variations, including

00:07:53.960 --> 00:07:58.831
one from Ming dynasty scholar Yang Shen, who wrote in this text [Sheng’an waiji]) that

00:07:58.831 --> 00:08:03.780
taotie is one of the nine children of the
dragon, who “liked to eat and drink” to

00:08:03.780 --> 00:08:04.780
excess.

00:08:04.780 --> 00:08:07.569
Different origin story, same defining traits.

00:08:07.569 --> 00:08:09.570
&nbsp;In The Guideways through Mountains and Seas

00:08:09.570 --> 00:08:14.460
or Classic of Mountains and Seas [Shanhaijing], a comprehensive text of Chinese mythology,

00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:20.020
geography, nature, and bestiary, we find another taotie- like being.

00:08:20.020 --> 00:08:22.260
&nbsp;In the text, there’s a creature called the

00:08:22.260 --> 00:08:23.260
Paoxiao.

00:08:23.260 --> 00:08:27.400
It has “a goat’s body with a human’s
face,” eyes behind its armpits, tiger’s

00:08:27.400 --> 00:08:28.900
teeth, and human hands.

00:08:28.900 --> 00:08:32.650
It “makes a sound like a baby” and is
a “man-eater.”

00:08:32.650 --> 00:08:35.050
Quite the combination of features.

00:08:35.050 --> 00:08:39.830
Composed between the 4th and 1st centuries BCE, all of the strange creatures depicted

00:08:39.830 --> 00:08:45.279
in this comprehensive text were believed to actually exist, and it was us humans’ job

00:08:45.279 --> 00:08:47.550
to live among them safely.

00:08:47.550 --> 00:08:52.780
While the earliest taotie bronze images don’t seem to resemble this monster at all, Chinese

00:08:52.780 --> 00:08:57.210
philosopher and scholar Guo Pu (276-324 C.E.), considered an expert on the text, insists

00:08:57.210 --> 00:09:02.020
that this is the same Taotie mentioned in Zuo’s Commentary, leading many scholars

00:09:02.020 --> 00:09:03.180
to agree.

00:09:03.180 --> 00:09:08.279
Outside of Chinese relics, Yu-Gi-Oh, and a few video games, physical depictions of the

00:09:08.279 --> 00:09:10.620
Taotie are largely limited in modern culture.

00:09:10.620 --> 00:09:16.700
In fact, Taotie graced the silver screen for the first time in the 2016 film The Great

00:09:16.700 --> 00:09:17.700
Wall.

00:09:17.700 --> 00:09:22.300
Though it is portrayed as an alien reptilian species with dark green skin and sharp teeth,

00:09:22.300 --> 00:09:28.110
the body markings are similar to the patterns seen in Ancient Bronze Chinese vessels, and

00:09:28.110 --> 00:09:31.029
the name “Tao Tei” is similar enough.

00:09:31.029 --> 00:09:33.110
&nbsp;We can roll our eyes at Matt Damon’s man

00:09:33.110 --> 00:09:38.970
bun, but in many ways, this movie is another example of how traditional monster legends

00:09:38.970 --> 00:09:42.810
can be adapted to fit modern narratives--and modern audiences.&nbsp;

00:09:42.810 --> 00:09:47.750
I&nbsp; also see some historical legitimacy in
the adaptation.

00:09:47.750 --> 00:09:53.750
The green skin reminds me of the Neolithic jade figures with taotie-like decorative design.

00:09:53.750 --> 00:09:58.420
And some of the grunt Tao Tei soldiers have human-like hands, “‘tiger’s teeth,”

00:09:58.420 --> 00:10:03.290
annddd eyes near their shoulders, calling to mind the creature from Classic of Mountains

00:10:03.290 --> 00:10:04.640
and Seas.

00:10:04.640 --> 00:10:10.410
The queen of the species in the film even has horns—again paying homage to the early

00:10:10.410 --> 00:10:12.500
taotie bronze motifs.

00:10:12.500 --> 00:10:13.500
As for their motivation—pure Taotie.

00:10:13.500 --> 00:10:23.700
The director of the film [Zhang Yimou] has even acknowledged ancient inspiration for

00:10:23.700 --> 00:10:25.380
his gluttonous creatures.&nbsp;

00:10:25.380 --> 00:10:28.360
And gluttony is a timeless theme.

00:10:28.360 --> 00:10:34.450
The Taotie arose from visual motifs—not only oral or written stories, which not only

00:10:34.450 --> 00:10:40.870
makes its original evolution unique, but demonstrates how the evolution of mythology and monsters

00:10:40.870 --> 00:10:42.550
is fluid.

00:10:42.550 --> 00:10:47.610
The Taotie is such a good example of how mythology is always changing.

00:10:47.610 --> 00:10:52.330
Visuals can construct meaning, and in this example, the evolution from art to monster

00:10:52.330 --> 00:10:56.870
by way of mythology can end with a timeless warning against over consumption.&nbsp;

